process_observations:
What worked well. Codex-executed MiniFly iterations 3-24 (13-14 Sep) are the high-water mark of audit practice. They:
- freeze protocols and hash-lock them before outcomes;
- keep all assigned cases in denominators, with eligibility audits so an arm cannot win by having fewer eligible old memories;
- use the history as the inferential unit;
- run startup invariants and independent replays;
- log amendments with every rerun retained;
- add 'what this does not establish' sections;
- stop at failed gates without fallback sweeps;
- separate engineering scorecards from biology scorecards and refuse promotion when either fails.

Version naming was chaotic and caused evidence-identity problems:
- V-numbers collide across lines (V50-V55 byte/BANC vs MiniFly V65=iteration10, V66=iteration11, a '61' label in a handoff);
- evolution labels (V80E/V81E/V82E) disagree between folders, designs and decision.json;
- quick and full runs share report formats.
A stable experiment ID scheme (lineage + immutable run ID) would have prevented several mislabels.

Quality of the late era (15-16 Sep: V83, V86, V87, RCE60, V88) shifts:
- runners were authored in a sandbox without the native backend;
- QA was done by mocks and hostile unit suites, with native validation deferred to the user's Mac;
- REPORT.md files became thin auto-templates with flags and file lists but no interpretation.
Hostile test suites improved determinism and plumbing, but in V88 they also enforced a narrowed objective. Tests verify the implementation against the design and cannot detect a drifted design.
